sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design (orchestrator, 2026-11-13, plan A; 2026-11-14 one ongoing thread per role, continued by default, effort per role through T3 provider instances; 2026-11-14 provider, model and effort per role from the group, the Project or the Gateway, checked against the copy's offer): a turn of a task that runs in a copy of its Project's default Instance. Swamp runs T3's own CLI in the copy as agent; stock T3 accepts only full-access MCP sessions it issues itself, so no T3 session leaves the container. The implementer and the reviewer each keep one T3 thread that later turns continue. 2026-11-14: a question ends the turn: Orbit stores it, then stops the run through the stock T3 server's own RPC, and the answer comes as the next turn.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (step 5, S-Pi b): the Pi turn on a task VM is removed with the Pi task loop; this is the only turn of task:turn.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-15 (task small fixes): the admission starts the subtask, todo to running; completion stays the caller's.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (group 5321, Operation 24161): Orbit stops a turn's run. A turn cut by its bound stops its own run in its phase, and the next turn stops the run of an earlier turn that is over in Orbit (timed out, cancelled, lost) before it settles it, so `operation:cancel` and a new turn stop a run.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (group 5321 evidence): every turn gets the group's title and brief first and Orbit's fixed copy rules, so no agent guesses the scope or leaves a command running past its turn.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (reopen): the caller can reopen the last completed subtask of an open group; it is the current subtask again and its turns continue its threads.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-16 (group 5425): the prompt gives the turn's clock, admission time and end; the result names the agent thread's id and the agent's bounded reply, so a driver reads a turn in one call.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-19 (group 6005): each role gets its own fixed prompt text; the implementer runs the task check before it hands off, the reviewer gets the check's results and the handoff note, and a re-review covers only what changed since the last finished review.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task:turn
  %% kind: tasks.copy_turn
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve
  participant N as Task copy<br/>incus exec · T3
  CLI->>GW: task:turn TASK<br/>--role · --thread
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=implementer --prompt="Run Doctor and commit a note."`
  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=implementer --prompt="Start over from the brief." --thread=new`
  %%> - `orbit task:turn SUBTASK --role=reviewer --prompt="Review the checked commit." --check=CHECK`
  Note over GW: Admit the turn<br/>owned copy · subtask · role thread
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - a group that owns a task copy, or whose Project has `nested_fleet` on, turns in its copy as `tasks.copy_turn`. Task VMs are removed: every other group answers `tasks.vm_runtime_removed` (422), and nothing is recorded. A nested-fleet group without a copy refuses here with `tasks.no_workspace`
  %%> - composes the prompt from the fixed text of the turn's role (docs/reference/tasks.md#run-an-external-turn): the group's title and brief in `<task_brief>`, the subtask's in `<subtask_brief>`, its deliverables in `<deliverables>` (left out when it has none), the role's rules, the caller's prompt in `<turn>`, then the line `Orbit ends this turn at END.`, where END is the admission's time in UTC plus the 3,600 s bound. No role gets a rule of the other role; the texts name nothing of one Project but its task check command. The 100000-character limit covers the whole text; a longer one refuses `tasks.prompt_too_large`, and nothing is recorded
  %%> - an implementer's rules: how to work in the copy, then before it hands off: check each deliverable, run the Project's task check and fix what it reports (left out, and the later steps numbered on, for a Project without one), give the asked proof with no stand-in or ask, clean up, commit. Its last message is the handoff note. It asks with the question tool; a question ends the turn, and the answer comes as the next turn
  %%> - a reviewer's text names its check, the check's commit and the base that check compared against (the start, or the tree of `git merge-tree --write-tree START HEAD` when the check merged the default head first), and each deliverable's result in that check (`passed`, `failed`, `not_checked`). It gives the implementer's handoff note: the reply of the latest finished implementer turn on the subtask that did not ask, as stored, or no block when there is none. Then the reviewer rule (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-reviewer-rule), the pull request text rule (docs/reference/tasks.md#the-reviewers-pull-request-text) and the fixed verdict line
  %%> - the first reviewer turn on a subtask gets the first-review lines; every later one gets the re-review lines, with its round and the commit of the latest finished reviewer turn on the subtask. A finished turn succeeded and did not ask; a reviewer turn that failed or was cancelled does not count, and one that asked finished no review, so the turn that answers it keeps its round. Orbit composes each turn from the records, so a thread that started under an older prompt gets a valid prompt
  %%> - a turn that continues a thread whose last turn asked quotes that question, each with its options, first inside `<turn>`, before the caller's prompt: the prompt is the answer. Its intent also names exactly those stored request ids, once per request
  %%> - without `--thread`, the turn continues its role's thread: the implementer's thread of the current subtask, or the group's reviewer thread, when it is a `t3` thread of this copy. A role with no such thread starts one. `--thread=new` starts a new one; `--thread=ID` must name the thread the turn would continue
  %%> - a new thread takes the role's provider, model and effort, each from the group, else the Project's default, else the Gateway's `ORBIT_TASKS_COPY_<ROLE>_PROVIDER`, `_MODEL` and `_EFFORT` (shipped: `claude`, `claude-opus-5-5`, `medium` for the implementer and `high` for the reviewer)
  %%> - the provider and the effort name the T3 provider instance that carries the effort, as `claudeMedium`; the thread records the driver `t3`, the copy's Node, the model, the effort and that instance
  %%> - a continued thread keeps its model and effort: T3 keeps the thread's provider instance
  %%> - a reviewer turn names the subtask's checked candidate with `--check`; the commit goes into the turn
  %%> - the current subtask moves from `todo` to `running` in the admission's transaction, under the group's row lock; a refused turn moves nothing. No turn moves it further: the caller completes it (docs/reference/tasks.md#subtask-status-in-a-task-copy), and may reopen the last one with `task:update SUBTASK --status=running`, which makes it the current subtask again (docs/reference/tasks.md#reopen-the-last-subtask)
  %%> - the intent names the copy's container, the T3 command, whether the turn starts or continues its thread, the provider instance, model and model options, the candidate, the 3,600 s bound and a title that names the Orbit thread; no credential and no vault key
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the group owns its copy and the copy is `active`; otherwise `tasks.no_workspace` or `tasks.copy_not_ready`, and nothing is recorded
  %%> - the copy's last completion did not fail; otherwise `tasks.copy_completion_failed` with its Operation, failed step, error code and the retry `orbit instance:complete ID`, and nothing is recorded
  %%> - the role is `implementer` or `reviewer`, and the request names no model: the role `consult` and a `model` were options of the removed Pi turn, and either answers `validation.failed` (422) on that field
  %%> - `--thread=ID` names the role's thread that the turn would continue; otherwise `tasks.thread_role_mismatch`
  %%> - a reviewer's check is the subtask's checked candidate; otherwise `tasks.commit_not_checked`
  %%> - a new thread's resolved provider, model and effort are ones the provider honours (docs/reference/tasks.md#copy-turn-agents): `claude` at `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` or `max`, `codex` at those or `ultra`, each with a model name; `antigravity` with one of its models at that model's efforts. Otherwise `tasks.agent_unsupported` (422) naming the field, the value, its source and what the provider accepts, and nothing is recorded
  %%> - a new thread's provider and effort name a provider instance that Orbit writes into the copy's T3 settings: one per Claude and Codex effort, and the one `antigravity` instance in a copy whose create settings hold it. Otherwise `tasks.copy_turn_unoffered` (422) with what the copy offers in `details.offered`, and nothing is recorded. Orbit owns this list, so the check reads nothing in the copy
  %%> - a new Antigravity thread starts on the tiered model `<model>-<effort>`, such as `gemini-4-neon-medium`; the intent also names the base model, which T3's catalog lists. A continued Antigravity thread whose first turn started on its bare model is refused with `tasks.copy_thread_untiered` (422): start a new thread with `--thread=new`
  %%> - no other Operation of the group runs; otherwise `operation.target_busy`
  %%> - one rule settles a turn: its run is over, and a reviewer's detach is undone. A thread is its captured T3 ID, whatever its title; only a turn with no ID that T3 holds is found by its title. A recorded run is over only when a readback shows that exact run in a status that ends it; an accepted interrupt, or a run or thread T3 does not show, is no proof. A turn with no recorded run is over when T3 lists its thread in no active state. A turn whose result shows that, or that a later turn's phase found so, is settled
  %%> - every turn of the group that started a run and is not settled goes into the intent, to be settled first: its Operation, its title, and for a reviewer's turn the task branch. A check, a publish or a proof of the copy cannot read T3: it answers `tasks.copy_turn_unended` with that Operation in `details.operation_id`, and waits for the next turn, which stops the earlier run and settles it. `operation:cancel` ends a running turn's Operation, and the next turn stops its run. When the last subtask is completed, the caller reopens it (docs/reference/tasks.md#reopen-the-last-subtask) and its next turn settles the copy; when a completed subtask cannot reopen, no turn can run and `task:cancel` is the only way out
  GW->>SW: Start one run<br/>operation-task-copy-turn
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>Operation recorded
  %%> Result:
  %%> - follow the Operation while Swamp runs the turn
  Note over SW: input
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes the copy's container, the SSH target and the turn into the private run folder
  SW->>N: Run the turn
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one Ansible phase on the copy's Node: `incus exec` into the copy, then `runuser -u agent`, non-login, with the agent's groups
  %%> - first one `true` in the copy: a copy the phase cannot reach fails before anything is launched, so its group stays free
  %%> - before any checkout or start, a new thread checks its model against the slugs and aliases in T3's cached `model-manifest.json` for the driver named in `settings.json`. A match passes without a provider request
  %%> - if the copy cannot read the provider instance's driver, the turn fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unverified` before checkout or launch
  %%> - if the catalog does not confirm a Codex model, the copy sends one bounded, tool-free request to its configured Codex Responses provider. A usable response must name the requested model exactly. A provider rejection fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unoffered`. An inconclusive probe fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unverified`. Both leave the thread unlaunched
  %%> - for other drivers, an unlisted model fails with `tasks.copy_turn_model_unoffered`; the listed slugs stay in the run folder's evidence, not in the error. Without a catalog for the driver, that check is skipped and the provider can reject the model in the run
  %%> - then each earlier turn to settle: `t3 thread list` finds its thread by the captured thread ID, by title only when no ID is recorded or listed. A thread that T3 still shows active has the earlier turn's captured run stopped first: `t3_copy_rpc.mjs stop THREAD RUN` interrupts exactly that run and waits for its end, and refuses another run; a turn with no captured run is not stopped and stays open. A thread in a state that runs nothing (the result step's list), none of that title, or whose run the stop ended or found ended, has ended; a state Orbit does not know leaves its turn open; the evidence names each stopped run. A reviewer's detach in effect is marked in the copy by the ref `refs/orbit/review-detach`, at the tip the branch had. Once every earlier run has ended and the mark is set, a still detached checkout is restored as after a reviewer's turn (reset, clean, the task branch); it must then be clean on that branch at the mark's tip, and the mark is cleared. Without the mark the tree is not touched. A stop that fails leaves its turn unsettled: nothing is touched or launched, and the phase fails with `tasks.copy_turn_unended`; the work in the copy stays
  %%> - a continued turn then finds its T3 thread by its title with `t3 thread list`. A list that fails or is not JSON fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed`; when T3 holds not exactly one thread of the title, the phase fails with `tasks.copy_thread_missing`. Nothing is launched
  %%> - before any reviewer checkout or launch, a continued turn that answers stored questions runs `t3_copy_rpc.mjs answered THREAD REQUEST...`. In one RPC session it cancels exactly those pending user-input requests with `runtime-request.respond`, decision `cancel`, without sending a reply message, then reads them back. Requests already resolved, cancelled or absent need no action. An intent admitted before `answered` was added treats its absence as an empty list. If a stored request stays pending, or its resolution cannot be confirmed, the phase fails with `tasks.copy_question_unresolved` and launches nothing. Interrupting a T3 run alone does not resolve every provider's questions
  %%> - a reviewer turn then requires a clean checkout on a branch, reads the branch's tip, sets the copy's detach mark at it and checks the candidate out, detached; a checkout with changes fails with `tasks.copy_tree_dirty` before anything is launched. The detach mark is written as soon as the checkout is detached
  %%> - a new thread's model options: a Codex thread carries its effort as the `reasoningEffort` option, since T3 0.1.7 sends Codex no other effort; a Claude thread carries none, and its instance's `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` sets its effort
  %%> - a new thread is created first: `t3_copy_rpc.mjs create TITLE INSTANCE MODEL OPTIONS` makes it in T3's project at `/srv/workspace`, in full access, with no message, through T3's own RPC; stock `t3 thread start` takes no model options. A create that makes no thread runs nothing and fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed` and T3's message
  %%> - the run: `t3 thread send --wait --json THREAD -` on the created or continued thread. The prompt goes on stdin, bounded by `timeout` at 3,600 s
  %%> - a wait that the bound cut (`timeout` exits 124, or 137 after the 30 s kill grace) leaves T3's run going. The start printed nothing, so Orbit does not know the run's ID and stops no run: the turn holds until T3 lists its thread in a state that runs nothing, or `task:cancel`
  %%> - `t3 thread list` finds the thread the turn sent to by its ID, also when the bound cut the wait or a person renamed it; only a turn that made no thread is looked for by its title. `t3 thread show --json` reads it back, and `t3_copy_rpc.mjs usage THREAD` reads the token use T3 keeps on each provider turn of the thread from `orchestration.getThreadProjection`; a usage read that fails leaves the use unknown and fails nothing. Each read is bounded at 60 s
  %%> - after a reviewer turn whose run is over, also a failed one, the phase discards what the reviewer changed and checks the branch out again, then reads that the checkout is clean, on its branch, and that the branch is at the tip read before the detach; a verified restore clears the copy's detach mark. A run that may still write, such as a cut one T3 lists running, is not restored: the mark stays, and the next turn restores the checkout once it proves the run over
  %%> - a run that waits for an answer (`--wait` exits 2): first `t3_copy_rpc.mjs read` stores the thread's pending requests, each question with its id, header, text and options as the agent asked them. Only when every request is a question stored whole, and only after that evidence is written, `t3_copy_rpc.mjs interrupt` stops that run with T3's `run.interrupt` and waits for it to end within the helper's 42 s deadline. It stops the run only while it waits for exactly the stored requests and every request node belongs to that run: a run that someone answered, or that now asks something else, goes on. Each call is bounded at 60 s
  %%> - `t3_copy_rpc.mjs stop THREAD` reads the thread, finds its active run, sends the same `run.interrupt` as `interrupt` and waits for the run's end within the same 42 s deadline. It answers whether the run stopped, its id and its last state; a thread with no active run answers `stopped: false` with no run. Both commands share one interrupt path
  %%> - the helper runs on the copy's Node.js runtime, from stdin. It speaks the stock T3 server's own WebSocket RPC (`orchestration.getThreadProjection`, `orchestration.dispatchCommand`) with a session from `t3 auth session issue`: read-only for the read, one minute, held only in the helper's memory, revoked before it exits. T3 stays stock
  %%> - writes each evidence file as soon as its command ends: the thread found to continue, the reviewer's checkout, the detach mark, the launch mark, the settle's stops, the start's end, the question, the interrupt, the stop of a cut run, the read, the restore; a cut phase still tells whether a turn was launched
  %%> - T3's CLI issues its own full-access session inside the copy and revokes it when it exits; the replies stay in the run folder, never in a log
  Note over N: Stored requests<br/>resolve · send
  Note over N: T3 runs the turn<br/>provider instance · effort
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - the copy's T3 settings hold one `claudeAgent` provider instance per effort level, `claudeLow` to `claudeMax`, each with `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL` in its environment; the copy's create writes them
  %%> - Claude Code takes that variable over the `--effort` T3 passes, so the thread runs at its recorded effort: verified with Claude Code 2.1.311 and T3 0.1.7
  Note over SW: result
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - maps the run's status: `completed` is done, `waiting` asks for input, any other status fails with `tasks.turn_failed`, a cut wait fails with `tasks.turn_timeout`, a start that T3 refused fails with `tasks.copy_turn_failed`, a thread to continue that T3 does not hold fails with `tasks.copy_thread_missing`, a stored answered request whose resolution is not confirmed fails before launch with `tasks.copy_question_unresolved`
  %%> - a turn that asks keeps its question in the result. Its run stopped, it is `asking_for_input` and over. A question Orbit could not read fails with `tasks.copy_question_unread`, and a run Orbit could not stop with `tasks.copy_question_unstopped`; neither is over
  %%> - names the provider instance, the model and the effort the thread started with, as the admission recorded them
  %%> - a reviewer turn with the detach mark and no restore that reads clean on its branch at its tip fails with `tasks.copy_review_unrestored`, whatever its run did, also when it failed or nothing was launched; the error keeps the run's own error
  %%> - keeps the message text of the thread, all its turns, redacted and bounded to 256 KiB with the newest kept; T3's CLI gives no tool items. A user message keeps only its ID, author and time, never its text: redaction does not catch an ordinary private prompt
  %%> - names `sent` from the launch mark and `launched` only when the start reported the run it started; a send with no reported run is possibly sent. Each stored question names the run that owns its request as `run_id`; a read with a request of no owner stores no question, stops no run and fails with `tasks.copy_question_unread`. A start that printed no status names no end. The error keeps no text of T3 or a provider and nothing parsed from it. A run status outside T3's known statuses is not named. `ended` follows one rule on the turn's own run, row by row as the result step's `over()` names them: positive evidence of that run's end, never another run of the thread; a question not stored with its owner run or not confirmed stopped keeps the hold `spent` stays empty: the CLI driver proves no earlier run
  %%> - records whether the turn is over: nothing was launched, the start printed an end other than a wait for an answer, T3 lists the thread the turn sent to, by its ID, in no active state, or, for a turn that made no thread, holds none of its title. A thread of the turn's ID that T3 does not list proves nothing. An unrestored reviewer turn is never over
  %%> - names the earlier turns it found settled; the Gateway counts them settled from then on
  %%> - names `usage`, the thread's token use summed over all its provider turns: `input_tokens` is T3's `inputTokens` less `cachedInputTokens`, `cached_input_tokens` is `cachedInputTokens`, `output_tokens` is `outputTokens`, `tokens` is `inputTokens` plus `outputTokens`, and `model_calls` the count of provider turns. No provider turn, a provider turn without `usageStatus` `complete`, or a failed read is null: unknown, never zeros
  Note over SW: cleanup
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the private run folder
  Note over SW: outcome
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - native assert: the turn is done, or it asked and its run stopped
  alt the turn is done or asked
    SW-->>GW: Native terminal result<br/>capture result
    Note over GW: Record the thread<br/>state · question · transcript
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the thread's state and error, T3's thread id as its external id, and the transcript in the Gateway's archive: `task:agent:show` reads it after the copy is gone
    %%> - the external id's admission: the ID that capture set stays `admitted` until T3 reports it back, which makes it `confirmed`; a reported ID that replaced it, or any report on a row from before the mark, is `overwritten`
    %%> - only a `launched` turn replaces the thread's questions; a thread whose stored request IDs are exactly the result's `spent` asks nothing any more
    %%> - the archive keeps a user message only by its ID, author and time, also when an older worker sends its text
    %%> - the error is the fixed sentence of its code, plus a fixed sentence that the group holds when the turn has not ended, never the worker's text; no diagnostic field is kept; a question without its owner run is not stored, records `tasks.copy_question_unread` unless the turn is `tasks.copy_review_unrestored`, and keeps the hold; a code outside the copy turn's codes is `tasks.copy_turn_failed`
    %%> - a turn that asked keeps its question on the thread: `task:agent:list` shows it until the thread's next turn. The answer is the next `task:turn` of the role, or a message to the thread from a T3 client
    %%> - the result names the provider instance, the model and the effort the thread started with, from its record
    %%> - the result names `agent_id`, the agent thread's row that `task:agent:show GROUP AGENT_ID` reads, and `reply`: the text of the transcript's last `assistant` entry, its first 16,384 characters, then ` [cut: N more characters; read task:agent:show GROUP AGENT_ID]` when it is longer. No assistant entry, such as a transcript of user entries and Orbit's truncation notice only: `reply` is null. The transcript stays out of the result
    %%> - a result's `usage` replaces the thread's `tokens`, `input_tokens`, `cached_input_tokens`, `output_tokens` and `model_calls`: it is the T3 thread's whole use, also for a continued thread. A null or absent `usage` leaves them as they are
  else the turn failed or ran past its bound
    SW--xGW: Native failed result<br/>error code
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the thread is `failed` with T3's status as its error, and the transcript T3 had
    %%> - the result names `agent_id` and `reply` as for a turn that is done
    %%> - a turn cut by its bound whose run Orbit stopped is over. A cancelled turn, a lost phase, or a cut turn whose stop failed may still run in the copy: the next turn stops its run, then settles it once T3 shows its run over and a reviewer's checkout is clean on the task branch. That turn fails with `tasks.copy_turn_unended` only when a stop fails or the checkout stays unclean. The work stays in the copy. `task:cancel` still ends it with the copy
  end
  loop until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show<br/>Operation number
  end
